WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.669 --> 00:00:06.020
MALLABY: This is normally called the WEU World
Economic Update. Today, it's called the NDU,

00:00:06.020 --> 00:00:12.599
the Non-Davos Update. We promise that there
is no ice outside, at least until this evening,

00:00:12.599 --> 00:00:17.310
that there is no jet lag for most of you,
that you will not slip on the sidewalk as

00:00:17.310 --> 00:00:21.600
you get out, and it's a lot less expensive
here, OK? So be grateful.

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:27.619
We have a great panel to survey the latest
in the world economy. Lewis Alexander, frequently

00:00:27.619 --> 00:00:34.851
on this show. Philippe Legrain in the middle,
who comes from the London School of Economics

00:00:34.851 --> 00:00:39.629
and was until recently an economic adviser
to President Barroso, the former President

00:00:39.629 --> 00:00:46.219
of the European Commission. And Michael Levi
at the end, who is -- directs the Maurice

00:00:46.219 --> 00:00:53.239
Greenberg Center for Geoeconomic Studies and
is the CFR resident expert on energy, climate,

00:00:53.239 --> 00:00:59.429
and much else.
So I think we'll start here with Lewis. On

00:00:59.429 --> 00:01:08.020
a kind of big-picture world economy assessment,
the IMF just revised down its growth forecast

00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:15.530
for 2015 slightly. The IMF always seems to
revise down its growth forecast. But anyway.

00:01:15.530 --> 00:01:22.689
It's a slight downward revision, but it's
still higher. The projection is now 3.5 percent,

00:01:22.689 --> 00:01:28.689
which is more growth than there was last year.
Equally, the markets have been very volatile.

00:01:28.689 --> 00:01:34.030
But the last time I checked the MFCI global
index last week, it was still up on the -- on

00:01:34.030 --> 00:01:37.969
the year, marginally.
So the question is, there's a lot of nervousness

00:01:37.969 --> 00:01:41.790
out there. But, you know, in a big-picture
sense, where are we? Is this a good time or

00:01:41.790 --> 00:01:47.170
a bad time for the world economy?
ALEXANDER: I think it's a very mixed time.

00:01:47.170 --> 00:01:49.970
One of the things you didn't mention is they
actually revised to up the forecast for the

00:01:49.970 --> 00:01:54.009
U.S. And I believe it was the only country
-- only major country which they did. And

00:01:54.009 --> 00:01:59.180
that highlights one of the challenges that
we face. It is -- we face an unusual degree

00:01:59.180 --> 00:02:04.159
of divergence between the United States on
the one hand and where policy is going there,

00:02:04.159 --> 00:02:07.740
and what's going on in rest of the world.
I think there's a lot of questions about what

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:14.930
does oil mean. The general view is that there
are more oil importers than there are oil

00:02:14.930 --> 00:02:19.280
exporters. And so in some general sense, the
fact that we've had a kind of supply shock

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:23.720
is a good thing for the global economy. But
there are a lot of questions about that. I

00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:27.830
think the research would tell you that the
benefits for oil importers aren't always as

00:02:27.830 --> 00:02:32.190
great as the cost for the others. And I think
that's part of the tension you're seeing.

00:02:32.190 --> 00:02:35.540
I think there's also a lot of concern about
what are the sort of financial consequences

00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:39.830
that come with that. I think in addition to
that, you've got major parts of the global

00:02:39.830 --> 00:02:44.159
economy that are suffering with serious risks
of deflation and serious questions about the

00:02:44.159 --> 00:02:50.830
effectiveness of policy. That's certainly
true in Europe. That's certainly true in Japan.

00:02:50.830 --> 00:02:56.390
And so I think it's kind of a -- it's a nervous
time. And that's what markets are reflecting.

00:02:56.390 --> 00:03:02.310
MALLABY: You mentioned that the U.S. was the
one economy to be revised upward. That's something

00:03:02.310 --> 00:03:07.510
that President Obama celebrated last night
in his speech. He also made it clear that

00:03:07.510 --> 00:03:15.590
he wants to make a push, notwithstanding the
hue of the Congress, for some sort of redistributive

00:03:15.590 --> 00:03:19.790
policy.
And I'm just wondering whether there's a potential

00:03:19.790 --> 00:03:25.720
irony here about the timing of that push,
in the following sense: it's true of course

00:03:25.720 --> 00:03:32.069
that median wages have been stagnating, and
the gains from growth have not been flaring

00:03:32.069 --> 00:03:36.840
down, but if you had to pick a moment in the
last five or maybe ten years where there were

00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:41.730
some signs that that might soften, that problem,
I would guess that is now.

00:03:41.730 --> 00:03:46.170
Let me just see how you -- how you react to
this. First is that the labor market has tightened

00:03:46.170 --> 00:03:52.700
quite a lot. And that normally portends some
upward potential for wages. And in the Financial

00:03:52.700 --> 00:03:58.810
Times this morning, there is a report precisely
of that effect occurring in the UK.

00:03:58.810 --> 00:04:04.390
Second is that healthcare inflation has been
slowing. And that should feed through into

00:04:04.390 --> 00:04:08.319
other forms of compensation.
And the third is that there's obviously a

00:04:08.319 --> 00:04:13.079
positive distributional effect from softer
oil prices.

00:04:13.079 --> 00:04:17.120
So for all these reasons, I'm just wondering
whether, you know, the President has pushed

00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:22.970
on the redistribution issue, when it's actually
less urgent than it has been.

00:04:22.970 --> 00:04:27.570
ALEXANDER: I guess I'd spin that in a slightly
different way. And partly what I would say

00:04:27.570 --> 00:04:33.001
is when you have a cyclical problem, obviously,
that's in some sense the first priority. I

00:04:33.001 --> 00:04:38.490
think in a certain extent, ever since the
recession in 2008 and 2009, the overwhelming

00:04:38.490 --> 00:04:42.680
priority has been let's sort of deal with
the cyclical shortfall.

00:04:42.680 --> 00:04:48.900
I think the fact that we are -- we still have
this problem of distribution is an indication

00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:52.920
that this is a fundamental structural problem
that, frankly, cyclical recovery can't solve

00:04:52.920 --> 00:04:56.490
by itself.
If you actually look at the late 1990s -- which

00:04:56.490 --> 00:05:00.750
was the last period when we really had a sort
of what I would describe as a high-pressure

00:05:00.750 --> 00:05:05.410
economy in the United States, unemployment
rate sort of well below NAIRU for an extended

00:05:05.410 --> 00:05:11.670
period of time -- you did see some moderation
of these divergent trends on income distribution,

00:05:11.670 --> 00:05:16.840
but only to a realty relatively modest degree.
I think to a certain extent the -- you know,

00:05:16.840 --> 00:05:20.990
the way this issue has (inaudible) up and
persisted is an indication that this is a

00:05:20.990 --> 00:05:27.010
deep structural problem, and a cyclical recovery
by itself, while it will help, will not solve.

00:05:27.010 --> 00:05:31.060
And so, you know, in some sense, you have
to turn to that -- you have to turn to other

00:05:31.060 --> 00:05:34.820
policies to address that.
MALLABY: Maybe I could go to Michael next.

00:05:34.820 --> 00:05:40.530
Because Lewis was talking earlier about the
way that there's a mixed impact from oil on

00:05:40.530 --> 00:05:45.210
the U.S. economy. And maybe you could flesh
that out a bit. How do you see the pluses

00:05:45.210 --> 00:05:51.460
and minuses?
LEVI: So first, at the global level -- obviously

00:05:51.460 --> 00:05:56.130
the globe is not an oil importer or an oil
exporter. So the question that you have to

00:05:56.130 --> 00:06:03.020
ask is, how does a shock to shift revenues
from countries and people who are likely to

00:06:03.020 --> 00:06:07.340
spend them to those that are less likely to
spend them or from those that are less likely

00:06:07.340 --> 00:06:10.912
to spend them.
And the general sense is that a fall in the

00:06:10.912 --> 00:06:15.660
oil price shifts money from those who are
less likely to spend, both across the global

00:06:15.660 --> 00:06:19.150
economy and within individual economies, to
those who are more likely to. And that's the

00:06:19.150 --> 00:06:23.360
basic sense for why people think this is generally
a boost.

00:06:23.360 --> 00:06:26.930
When you look at the U.S. economy, there are
three basic pieces. So the first is how does

00:06:26.930 --> 00:06:34.050
this affect producers? And historically, the
basic sense has been that this helps producers

00:06:34.050 --> 00:06:37.910
overall a bit, because they have cheaper inputs,
but that this isn't the main channel through

00:06:37.910 --> 00:06:42.930
which oil prices affect the economy. Now,
we have a very different situation because

00:06:42.930 --> 00:06:47.640
so much investment in the United States in
recent years has been in the oil and gas sector

00:06:47.640 --> 00:06:54.120
and supply chain. So I think people see this
as being a direct hit to producers.

00:06:54.120 --> 00:06:58.320
The second is that it puts more money in people's
pockets. And there the big question is, do

00:06:58.320 --> 00:07:04.870
they spend it? And those who think that this
will provide a decent boost to the U.S. economy

00:07:04.870 --> 00:07:09.360
tend to imagine that they will spend a decent
piece of that, which will help the economy

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:11.770
more broadly.
And I think the big question mark, particularly

00:07:11.770 --> 00:07:16.840
if you're looking at the historical data and
trying to extrapolate it to the future, is

00:07:16.840 --> 00:07:21.390
what's the role of monetary policy? A lot
of the work going back -- a lot of the work

00:07:21.390 --> 00:07:24.780
is very ambiguous. If you go back over the
last forty years and people try to figure

00:07:24.780 --> 00:07:33.270
out what changes in oil prices do to the economy,
it's very ambiguous. But when analysts have

00:07:33.270 --> 00:07:39.001
large estimates of the impact of a big change
in oil prices on the overall economy, they

00:07:39.001 --> 00:07:43.141
tend to include a large component that has
to do with the central bank acting. And in

00:07:43.141 --> 00:07:48.850
the case of falling oil prices, that means
using the flexibility created by falling oil

00:07:48.850 --> 00:07:53.960
prices to have more accommodating monetary
policy. And there's a reason -- and I think

00:07:53.960 --> 00:07:56.750
the others on the panel can speak more to
this, to be skeptical that this will have

00:07:56.750 --> 00:08:01.610
a significant impact in that direction.
MALLABY: Well, I mean, let's just follow that

00:08:01.610 --> 00:08:06.640
thought through for a second. So, you know,
clearly, you're right. The transmission mechanism

00:08:06.640 --> 00:08:13.280
from cheaper oil, less price pressure to growth
is partly through looser monetary policy.

00:08:13.280 --> 00:08:18.600
So the question must be where is that scope
for effective loosening? So in the U.S. case,

00:08:18.600 --> 00:08:24.750
it could show up in -- in a decision not to
tighten so early. So it seems like presumably

00:08:24.750 --> 00:08:28.980
there is more potential feedthrough there
than, say, for example, in Europe where they're

00:08:28.980 --> 00:08:33.779
going to do QE probably anyway. We'll get
to that in a second. And there's a doubt about

00:08:33.779 --> 00:08:37.930
how effective that will be. Or in Japan, where
they are kind of pulling the monetary trigger

00:08:37.930 --> 00:08:42.899
as much as they can already. Can you --
LEVI: There are other -- there are other limits

00:08:42.899 --> 00:08:49.670
in most places to the decision to loosen.
And, again, if you look historically, the

00:08:49.670 --> 00:08:54.400
case that you really want to draw on is 1986.
So you have two different kinds of oil price

00:08:54.400 --> 00:08:59.580
falls. You have ones that are triggered by
-- primarily by weak global economy, and ones

00:08:59.580 --> 00:09:03.220
that are triggered primarily by an unexpected
increase in supply. And the current one is

00:09:03.220 --> 00:09:07.959
a mix of those. But there's a substantial
part that's actually an increase in supply,

00:09:07.959 --> 00:09:11.990
rather than a weak global economy. And you
have to go back to 1986 to see the last example

00:09:11.990 --> 00:09:15.540
of that.
And when people look at the macroeconomic

00:09:15.540 --> 00:09:20.670
impact of the oil price fall in 1986, they
tend to see that it's considerably weaker

00:09:20.670 --> 00:09:25.080
than one might expect if you just sort of
took a generic impact of an oil price drop.

00:09:25.080 --> 00:09:33.019
1986 -- 1985, 1986, growth was actually weaker
than in 1984, despite a collapse in the oil

00:09:33.019 --> 00:09:38.670
price of about 50 percent.
And in that case, there actually is a considerable

00:09:38.670 --> 00:09:43.350
movement from the Federal Reserve to lower
interest rates, take advantage of the opportunity,

00:09:43.350 --> 00:09:48.200
boost the economy. But it still comes out
with weaker growth in those years.

00:09:48.200 --> 00:09:52.889
MALLABY: I want to come to Philippe in a second
about the QE in Europe. But Lewis, you had

00:09:52.889 --> 00:09:53.889
a quick --
(CROSSTALK)

00:09:53.889 --> 00:09:59.050
ALEXANDER: Yeah. Just a -- just a quick observation.
I think a big part of why long-run interest

00:09:59.050 --> 00:10:03.720
rates in the U.S. are where they are is because
what's happened to oil has affected the market's

00:10:03.720 --> 00:10:11.279
expectations for the pace of tightening. And
so yes, while we are at the zero lower bound,

00:10:11.279 --> 00:10:14.529
there's an expectation over the course of
the next several years that the Fed will be

00:10:14.529 --> 00:10:19.209
raising rates. And I do think what's happened
to the price of oil has affected those expectations.

00:10:19.209 --> 00:10:24.480
So I would argue at least for the U.S. case,
there is a significant offset that, frankly,

00:10:24.480 --> 00:10:28.740
we're already seeing in terms of these here
financial conditions coming centrally through

00:10:28.740 --> 00:10:29.740
long-run --
(CROSSTALK)

00:10:29.740 --> 00:10:33.660
MALLABY: So the U.S. is getting a monetary
feedthrough from oil prices.

00:10:33.660 --> 00:10:37.670
Philippe, what about Europe? You're skeptical
about -- you think monetary policy is basically

00:10:37.670 --> 00:10:40.230
stuck there, right?
LEGRAIN: So I just wanted to make one quick

00:10:40.230 --> 00:10:44.970
point on the global outlook. Lewis is absolutely
right that the U.S. is the only major economy

00:10:44.970 --> 00:10:49.080
that is strengthening, while most of the rest
of the world is slowing. And the rest of the

00:10:49.080 --> 00:10:53.649
word is tempted, therefore, to try and push
down its currency in order to compensate.

00:10:53.649 --> 00:10:56.980
Which brings about two questions. First of
all, how high can the U.S. dollar go, and

00:10:56.980 --> 00:11:02.499
two, relatedly, to what extent is the United
States willing and able to resume its role

00:11:02.499 --> 00:11:06.589
of pre-crisis, of being the kind of consumer
of first and last resort? And I think that

00:11:06.589 --> 00:11:10.360
is really crucial for determining at the global
outlook.

00:11:10.360 --> 00:11:16.839
In terms of the ECB's expected decision tomorrow
on QE, I think, you know, the ECB is massively

00:11:16.839 --> 00:11:25.490
behind the curve. We now have falling prices
in the Eurozone. And it's going to take a

00:11:25.490 --> 00:11:31.940
commitment to do whatever it takes in order
to shift inflation expectations, which have

00:11:31.940 --> 00:11:35.779
become unanchored.
And the fact is is that while it's a big step

00:11:35.779 --> 00:11:39.180
forward that the ECB is going to announce
QE -- and it's probably likely to be around

00:11:39.180 --> 00:11:46.269
500 billion euros and it'll probably be in
proportion to each country's capital cut contributions

00:11:46.269 --> 00:11:52.300
-- that Mario Draghi, even if he wanted to,
is not in a position to make a commitment

00:11:52.300 --> 00:11:59.870
to do whatever it takes, an unlimited commitment
that is needed. And therefore, I think that

00:11:59.870 --> 00:12:05.559
QE is going to disappoint, and that ultimately,
the Eurozone is sinking into a deflationary

00:12:05.559 --> 00:12:10.100
debt trap.
MALLABY: So you're making a point here that

00:12:10.100 --> 00:12:16.110
the efficacy of QE in some sense is kind of
a faith healing game. And if the faith healer

00:12:16.110 --> 00:12:22.139
is saying, you know, expect me to, you know,
pump up asset markets and stimulate the economy

00:12:22.139 --> 00:12:28.199
with this policy, but the faith healer's assistant
-- namely Germany -- is saying don't believe

00:12:28.199 --> 00:12:32.390
him, don't believe him, don't believe him
-- the expectations are not going to work.

00:12:32.390 --> 00:12:36.949
So that's one problem. But isn't there another
one about just the nature of the financial

00:12:36.949 --> 00:12:41.700
transmission mechanism in Europe? You don't
have deep capital markets. You do have banks,

00:12:41.700 --> 00:12:45.910
but banks face regulatory burdens right now.
LEGRAIN: Yeah. I'm skeptical about how effective

00:12:45.910 --> 00:12:52.930
QE has been, even in the United States and
the UK and Japan. I think it's inflated asset

00:12:52.930 --> 00:12:58.160
prices and not so much had an impact on real
investment. You know, it's easier to make

00:12:58.160 --> 00:13:03.740
money from financial speculation than from
taking risks in the real economy.

00:13:03.740 --> 00:13:09.470
But that's the case even with a government
that is fully supportive of the central bank.

00:13:09.470 --> 00:13:18.050
In the Eurozone, we have the ECB going ahead
with limited QE, conditional QE, with the

00:13:18.050 --> 00:13:24.790
most powerful country in the Eurozone, Germany,
vocal in its opposition. And therefore, it's

00:13:24.790 --> 00:13:30.269
doing it with one hand tied behind its back.
And it certainly isn't going to be able to

00:13:30.269 --> 00:13:35.009
deliver the whatever-it-takes commitment needed
to shift expectations.

00:13:35.009 --> 00:13:39.439
Had we done this earlier, perhaps. But, you
know, I think we're so far behind the curve

00:13:39.439 --> 00:13:43.029
that you need something bigger and bolder
than what's going to happen.

00:13:43.029 --> 00:13:46.430
MALLABY: Lewis, are you equally pessimistic?
I mean, you could argue that in the Japanese

00:13:46.430 --> 00:13:52.639
case, at the beginning of Abenomics, the first
reports on the success were actually quite

00:13:52.639 --> 00:13:56.930
positive. I mean, the central bank commitment
to double the size of its balance sheet had

00:13:56.930 --> 00:14:04.839
some effect. The problem was that then consumption
taxes were raised, and that killed the recovery.

00:14:04.839 --> 00:14:08.920
So doesn't that example suggest that even
when you're behind the curve -- maybe Philippe

00:14:08.920 --> 00:14:12.529
wants to come back on this. Even when you're
behind the curve, the central bank can make

00:14:12.529 --> 00:14:17.670
a difference if it is properly supported,
as Abe was supporting and, indeed, urging

00:14:17.670 --> 00:14:20.300
the bank of Japan to do this, then it does
work.

00:14:20.300 --> 00:14:25.800
The problem in Europe is that you don't have
the equivalent of sort of Abe. The political

00:14:25.800 --> 00:14:30.121
support is not there for -- for Draghi.
ALEXANDER: I agree very much with the way

00:14:30.121 --> 00:14:34.740
you characterize it. And look, I -- these
things are always difficult, and it's never

00:14:34.740 --> 00:14:39.770
totally clear how they're going to work.
But if you just look at Japan, there was QE

00:14:39.770 --> 00:14:44.620
more than fifteen years under Hayami, where
it was clear that the bank of Japan was -- wanted

00:14:44.620 --> 00:14:48.230
to get out of it as soon as they could. So
they kind of went into it, but they went into

00:14:48.230 --> 00:14:51.981
it very reluctantly. The difference between
that and what Kuroda has done, I think they

00:14:51.981 --> 00:14:56.339
very much understand the point you just made.
And I think the way you framed it is exactly

00:14:56.339 --> 00:15:02.019
right. If this is done in kind of a halfhearted
way without the clear commitment of the full

00:15:02.019 --> 00:15:08.809
ECB behind it -- so there's always this question
of how unlimited is it really, then it's much

00:15:08.809 --> 00:15:13.880
harder to see how it works.
I guess I'm perhaps a little more optimistic

00:15:13.880 --> 00:15:21.160
that we may be positively surprised this week.
We'll see. You're closer to it than I am.

00:15:21.160 --> 00:15:27.319
I guess I would argue in the U.S. case, I'm
perhaps a little more upbeat on what QE has

00:15:27.319 --> 00:15:31.279
done. One of the things I would stress is
that obviously in the wake of the crisis,

00:15:31.279 --> 00:15:35.370
a lot of what we were dealing with was balance
sheet adjustment. And if it did nothing else

00:15:35.370 --> 00:15:40.129
other than ease that, I think that was an
important contributor to sort of where we

00:15:40.129 --> 00:15:43.779
are. And so I guess I'm not so negative on
it.

00:15:43.779 --> 00:15:49.059
I do think the crucial question is is it plausible;
right? Ultimately, I agree that they're -- you

00:15:49.059 --> 00:15:54.499
know, Europe is facing very significant sort
of deflationary pressures, and it is going

00:15:54.499 --> 00:15:59.970
to take a substantial commitment -- you may
not be able to get quite to Kuroda's level.

00:15:59.970 --> 00:16:05.089
But something that's more than just a grudging
'we're doing this because we have to and we're

00:16:05.089 --> 00:16:08.199
going to get out of it as soon as we can'
kind of approach.

00:16:08.199 --> 00:16:13.589
MALLABY: I'm going to come to Greece in a
minute. But I want to first ask Michael something,

00:16:13.589 --> 00:16:19.879
which is that if you think back to sort of
the middle of last year, in the Eurozone,

00:16:19.879 --> 00:16:24.429
one of the things that would seem to be dragging
on growth more than one might have predicted

00:16:24.429 --> 00:16:31.369
if you just looked at the numbers, was that
the Ukraine crisis was affecting confidence

00:16:31.369 --> 00:16:37.240
in Germany and causing a sort of drying up
of corporate investment and a downward revision

00:16:37.240 --> 00:16:45.869
in German performance. Since then, Ukraine
is still in crisis and Russia has entered

00:16:45.869 --> 00:16:50.069
crisis, at least the currency crisis. There
may soon be a banking crisis.

00:16:50.069 --> 00:16:54.850
So what's your outlook if you -- if you just
-- can you sketch a bit how you see the Russia

00:16:54.850 --> 00:16:58.929
thing playing out, a classic oil economy?
And -- and then maybe we can talk about how

00:16:58.929 --> 00:17:02.160
that feeds back into Europe.
LEVI: And in some ways, worse than a classic

00:17:02.160 --> 00:17:06.840
oil economy. Because it's a classic oil economy
with these financial constraints coming out

00:17:06.840 --> 00:17:15.770
of sanctions. So I think you had the headlines
in December with the collapse of the ruble

00:17:15.770 --> 00:17:21.360
and this big focus on the currency side and
how the increase in Russian central bank interest

00:17:21.360 --> 00:17:27.060
rates had not succeeded in stemming that plunge.
And then there was this immediate focus on

00:17:27.060 --> 00:17:33.740
the currency side. Less focus, I think, than
probably is warranted on what will happen

00:17:33.740 --> 00:17:38.290
with the broader economy over the next couple
of years. A lot of the impact of what happened

00:17:38.290 --> 00:17:43.231
then will play out over the next couple years.
You put interest rates that high, it has a

00:17:43.231 --> 00:17:47.810
broad effect on the economy. So not -- so
essentially, distributes some of the pain

00:17:47.810 --> 00:17:51.960
that would normally be focused in the oil
and gas sector throughout the whole economy.

00:17:51.960 --> 00:17:56.450
And that inevitably has spillovers into Europe.
I think on the geopolitical front, that creates

00:17:56.450 --> 00:18:02.410
challenges in sustaining unity between the
United States and its European partners when

00:18:02.410 --> 00:18:07.310
it comes to maintaining or strengthening sanctions
against Russia.

00:18:07.310 --> 00:18:12.460
MALLABY: Philippe, do you want the comment
on that feedthrough from Russia into German

00:18:12.460 --> 00:18:16.750
and Europe?
LEGRAIN: Yeah. I think that the EU has been

00:18:16.750 --> 00:18:23.130
taken by surprise by the success of sanctions
in weakening the Russian economy. They think

00:18:23.130 --> 00:18:32.640
-- European policymakers saw them as almost
kind of symbolic. And now that it is causing

00:18:32.640 --> 00:18:38.060
the Russian economy to go into a tailspin
and potentially have a financial crisis, they

00:18:38.060 --> 00:18:43.910
are quite concerned because, obviously, Europe
is much more exposed economically to Russia

00:18:43.910 --> 00:18:49.750
than the United States is. And that's why
there's already talk about the EU potentially

00:18:49.750 --> 00:18:54.730
not renewing some of its sanctions when they
come up later this year. So I think, actually,

00:18:54.730 --> 00:18:59.021
sanctions have been a great success.
MALLABY: I want to go back to something that

00:18:59.021 --> 00:19:03.120
Philippe said earlier and put it to Michael
and maybe Lewis.

00:19:03.120 --> 00:19:08.450
So Philippe made the point that, you know,
the euro's already devalued a lot against

00:19:08.450 --> 00:19:14.400
the dollar. One of the channels through which
QE would function would be further devaluation

00:19:14.400 --> 00:19:18.880
of the euro against the dollar. We've seen
dramatic move against the Swiss franc, of

00:19:18.880 --> 00:19:23.480
course. And all of this is happening at a
time when the Japanese would also like to

00:19:23.480 --> 00:19:30.710
stimulate their economy by devaluing against
the dollar. China, with downwardly-revised

00:19:30.710 --> 00:19:38.640
growth, may be tempted to go back to a strategy
of inflating its current account surplus again.

00:19:38.640 --> 00:19:42.860
And so the question that Philippe raised,
which I think is a very good one, is what

00:19:42.860 --> 00:19:49.750
are the limits to tolerance in Washington
of everybody else trying to grow off the back

00:19:49.750 --> 00:19:55.940
of a strong dollar? Do you see there being
a policy reaction against this?

00:19:55.940 --> 00:20:02.820
LEVI: Yes. And I think it's particularly going
to be seen in the trade discussions that will

00:20:02.820 --> 00:20:05.841
be a focus this year.
I do want to highlight one thing that you

00:20:05.841 --> 00:20:12.710
mentioned, is the China piece of this. And
China has had its -- it's not pegged sharply

00:20:12.710 --> 00:20:17.450
to the U.S. currency, but it moves closely
with it. And so it's had its currency dragged

00:20:17.450 --> 00:20:23.670
along to some extent with the U.S. dollar
over the last however many months. And that's

00:20:23.670 --> 00:20:27.310
causing some pain and some concern. And so
that's something to watch additionally.

00:20:27.310 --> 00:20:32.410
But look, as you look at what's happening
in Washington, you saw the President try to

00:20:32.410 --> 00:20:38.490
turn Congress' focus to Transpacific Partnership
and asking Congress to give him trade promotion

00:20:38.490 --> 00:20:45.740
authority. My colleague, Rob Kahn, writes
and talks a lot about how these monetary issues

00:20:45.740 --> 00:20:52.060
are going to come in substantially in the
-- in the political debate over trade. I don't

00:20:52.060 --> 00:20:57.020
think that members of Congress or the American
people neatly separate these various dynamics

00:20:57.020 --> 00:21:00.830
-- traditional trade discussions, monetary
policy discussions -- when they think about

00:21:00.830 --> 00:21:05.220
how the global economy affects them. And if
they think that something's happening in the

00:21:05.220 --> 00:21:09.820
monetary space that they see is unfair and
they're being asked do enter a large trade

00:21:09.820 --> 00:21:14.110
agreement that is supposed to be a next-generation
trade agreement, they'll be -- there will

00:21:14.110 --> 00:21:19.240
be questions about why it doesn't deal with
the problems that are being seen on this front.

00:21:19.240 --> 00:21:25.250
MALLABY: Right. So -- so all this unbalanced
economy and trying to piggyback on the strong

00:21:25.250 --> 00:21:31.520
dollar is negative for the outlook on trade
agreements, even if President Obama were to

00:21:31.520 --> 00:21:35.000
get his trade promotion authority, which he
asked for yesterday.

00:21:35.000 --> 00:21:38.960
Lewis, you want to comment on that, the likely
political reaction and...

00:21:38.960 --> 00:21:42.510
(CROSSTALK)
ALEXANDER: Look, I -- I've been struck by,

00:21:42.510 --> 00:21:48.760
frankly, how well the currency issues have
been managed kind of at the G7 level so far.

00:21:48.760 --> 00:21:56.660
Obviously, that was a big component of Abenomics.
And I think the G7, you know, with a large

00:21:56.660 --> 00:22:01.810
input from the U.S., basically took the position
that as long as it was domestically focused

00:22:01.810 --> 00:22:06.810
and was not focused on the exchange rate,
having a very aggressive monetary policy,

00:22:06.810 --> 00:22:11.600
which had the consequence of a depreciating
exchange rate, was acceptable. And so far,

00:22:11.600 --> 00:22:16.980
I think that -- that has helped. I think frankly,
as long as the U.S. economy is doing reasonably

00:22:16.980 --> 00:22:21.060
well, I think that's a -- that is a kind of
formula that can work.

00:22:21.060 --> 00:22:27.450
Look, I agree, you know, trade agreements
are going to be important. And the circumstances

00:22:27.450 --> 00:22:31.100
for them are going to be different than they
were in the past. That was clear in the President's

00:22:31.100 --> 00:22:35.710
remarks last night. He put it in sort of a
very different context.

00:22:35.710 --> 00:22:42.230
I think -- I actually have been reasonably
pleasantly surprised at how all of this has

00:22:42.230 --> 00:22:48.130
been managed on the currency side. And so
I'm not overly worried about that, per se.

00:22:48.130 --> 00:22:53.680
I think we'll have to see how this goes. If
we get into sort of a situation where the

00:22:53.680 --> 00:22:59.690
dollar continues to go up very rapidly, you
start to see some obvious drag from that on

00:22:59.690 --> 00:23:05.340
the economy that is more than just some marginal
slowing, then it potentially becomes a bigger

00:23:05.340 --> 00:23:08.140
deal.
But the dynamics of this are really quite

00:23:08.140 --> 00:23:13.730
different. If you look at say, for example,
where are Japanese autos produced today versus

00:23:13.730 --> 00:23:18.260
where they were produced in the 1980s, they're
largely produced here. To the extent that

00:23:18.260 --> 00:23:23.070
it's an auto issue, it's an issue about parts.
It's not about the cars themselves. I think

00:23:23.070 --> 00:23:27.830
that has changed the dynamic. I think one
of the reasons you've been able to see Abenomics

00:23:27.830 --> 00:23:31.660
work without it generating those tensions
is precisely that fact.

00:23:31.660 --> 00:23:37.172
Now, unfortunately, one of the negative consequences
of that is actually the depreciation of the

00:23:37.172 --> 00:23:41.800
yen actually hasn't generated the benefits
for Japan that they had hoped. And that's,

00:23:41.800 --> 00:23:47.510
you know, one of the sort of flip sides of
all of this. But we'll see how this goes.

00:23:47.510 --> 00:23:52.220
I have been in some sense pleasantly surprised
that it hasn't been a bigger issue.

00:23:52.220 --> 00:23:55.832
MALLABY: I'm going to ask members to -- for
questions in a second. But I want to go to

00:23:55.832 --> 00:24:01.340
Greece before we stop. Because, of course,
Greece has on election this Sunday which,

00:24:01.340 --> 00:24:07.520
you know, on one view could be pretty dramatic.
And it's already shaken up the markets a bit.

00:24:07.520 --> 00:24:14.920
If Syriza were to be elected, it says it would
like to restructure the debt. Meanwhile, the

00:24:14.920 --> 00:24:20.390
European sort of center says it won't restructure
the debt. So we're heading for a fight that

00:24:20.390 --> 00:24:23.690
could be quite destabilizing.
But I think, Philippe, you've got maybe a

00:24:23.690 --> 00:24:27.040
different take on that.
LEGRAIN: Well, the first thing to say is that

00:24:27.040 --> 00:24:34.060
Syriza doesn't intend to renegotiate the privately-held
debt. It wants to renegotiate the debt owed

00:24:34.060 --> 00:24:40.030
to Eurozone governments. So markets shouldn't
panic too much about that.

00:24:40.030 --> 00:24:43.650
Second of all, that the fear that this is
going to lead to the Greece being forced out

00:24:43.650 --> 00:24:49.670
of the euro I think is exaggerated. Back in
2012 the German government seriously was considering

00:24:49.670 --> 00:24:54.940
trying to force Greece out. But that opinion
has changed. In any case, there's no legal

00:24:54.940 --> 00:24:59.750
provision for leaving the euro. It certainly
isn't up to Germany to decide. And it wouldn't

00:24:59.750 --> 00:25:04.580
be in Germany's interest. Because if Greece
leaves the euro, they basically lose all the

00:25:04.580 --> 00:25:09.270
money that is owed to them. Plus, there is
the (inaudible) effect of Greek exit potentially

00:25:09.270 --> 00:25:13.610
on other countries. Whereas if you do a deal
with Syriza on a partial rate write-down,

00:25:13.610 --> 00:25:19.290
you're going to make smaller losses.
Likewise, I don't think the ECB threat to

00:25:19.290 --> 00:25:23.410
cut off Greek banks is particularly credible.
I didn't think it was credible back in 2012

00:25:23.410 --> 00:25:28.020
either. I mean, if you think about it, the
ECB only exists because there's a currency

00:25:28.020 --> 00:25:33.850
called the euro. And they don't want to endanger
it and splinter it by ejecting Greece. And

00:25:33.850 --> 00:25:39.770
it would be very hard for unelected central
bankers to take such a political decision.

00:25:39.770 --> 00:25:44.410
So I think potentially the election of a Syriza
government clearly is going to lead to market

00:25:44.410 --> 00:25:50.720
turmoil. Clearly, there is difficult negotiations
ahead. Clearly, it could all go terribly wrong.

00:25:50.720 --> 00:25:55.810
At the same time, I think there is a scope
for a deal between the Eurozone governments

00:25:55.810 --> 00:26:00.430
and Greece on debt relief.
MALLABY: Well, let me push you a little bit

00:26:00.430 --> 00:26:08.430
on that. So as I understand it, the European
Central Bank holds some Greek government debt

00:26:08.430 --> 00:26:14.230
which was purchased when Trichet was president
and there was a limited program to purchase

00:26:14.230 --> 00:26:19.410
sovereign bonds. So some of these bonds are
going to mature in the next few months. And

00:26:19.410 --> 00:26:26.750
Syriza's position is we would like you to
roll them over. And the legal answer is, we

00:26:26.750 --> 00:26:31.430
can't. There's no program to roll these things
over. When they mature, you pay us the money,

00:26:31.430 --> 00:26:38.710
which the Greeks don't have.
So what I'm getting at that kind of in a sort

00:26:38.710 --> 00:26:44.760
of elastic political mindset, your kind of
game theory sound right. Right? The center

00:26:44.760 --> 00:26:50.570
loses more than it gains. But if the center
is in a legalistic mood -- and we should remember

00:26:50.570 --> 00:26:56.100
that the ECB was founded on the principles
of the highly-legalistic Bundesbank, isn't

00:26:56.100 --> 00:27:00.400
there a kind of potential for something that
is in nobody's interest to happen anyway?

00:27:00.400 --> 00:27:07.650
LEGRAIN: The ECB is a highly-political institution.
For an independent bank, it takes great care

00:27:07.650 --> 00:27:14.230
to -- about (inaudible) sensitivities. And
it certainly is aware of political consequences

00:27:14.230 --> 00:27:22.190
of its actions. So is that a card which is
part of the negotiations? Yes, of course it

00:27:22.190 --> 00:27:30.320
is. Is it going to be played with the disastrous
consequences that might arise? I doubt it.

00:27:30.320 --> 00:27:35.740
So in any case, you know, Syriza can find
money elsewhere. First of all, if they stop

00:27:35.740 --> 00:27:43.000
other debt repayment. Second of all, if they
raise more money through T bonds. Thirdly,

00:27:43.000 --> 00:27:50.100
by cutting other areas of spending. I think
there is -- while it could go wrong, I think

00:27:50.100 --> 00:27:55.120
there is scope for an agreement.
MALLABY: Do you feel equally optimistic, Lewis?

00:27:55.120 --> 00:28:02.470
ALEXANDER: Look, I -- I think we have gone
sort of down this road. We sorts of broached

00:28:02.470 --> 00:28:08.210
all of this in 2012, took a step back. This
thing that has been surprising to me in that

00:28:08.210 --> 00:28:13.320
whole process is the broad political commitment
across the euro to sort of stick with this,

00:28:13.320 --> 00:28:16.710
whatever it comes.
I do think there are challenges to getting

00:28:16.710 --> 00:28:23.760
there. But ultimately, I think as you've seen
the politics of this evolve in the euro area,

00:28:23.760 --> 00:28:28.540
nobody really wants this thing to break up.
And I think it will be difficult. There will

00:28:28.540 --> 00:28:32.280
be -- it will be noisy. But ultimately, they'll
find a solution.

00:28:32.280 --> 00:28:37.380
MALLABY: OK. So let's go to questions from
members. And please raise your hand if you

00:28:37.380 --> 00:28:43.030
have a question and say who you are. If there's
no questions, I will ask a question as well.

00:28:43.030 --> 00:28:52.181
But OK. We'll give you -- oh, I can see a
question there. Microphone coming. Remember

00:28:52.181 --> 00:28:57.590
this meeting is on the record. Yeah. Go ahead.
QUESTION: Thanks. I just had a question whether

00:28:57.590 --> 00:28:58.920
--
MALLABY: Can you say who you are --

00:28:58.920 --> 00:29:00.390
(CROSSTALK)
QUESTION: Oh, sorry. Gideon Lichfield from

00:29:00.390 --> 00:29:06.350
Quartz. Do you have an opinion on whether
the new head of monetary policy at the Russian

00:29:06.350 --> 00:29:10.980
central bank, who starts today, Tulin, has
a chance?

00:29:10.980 --> 00:29:15.080
MALLABY: Who wants to take that?
(LAUGHTER)

00:29:15.080 --> 00:29:22.970
(UNKNOWN): I have no idea.
MALLABY: Massive silence of up here. Lewis,

00:29:22.970 --> 00:29:26.290
your game.
ALEXANDER: Look, I think the challenges facing

00:29:26.290 --> 00:29:32.980
Russia at this point are -- are pretty severe.
Obviously, oil at where it is is a very different

00:29:32.980 --> 00:29:36.370
scenario.
Having said that, obviously, they have a lot

00:29:36.370 --> 00:29:40.730
more resources than they've had. So it partly
depends on exactly what version of the question

00:29:40.730 --> 00:29:45.630
you're asking. Look, I think if you look at
Russia's circumstances today relative to,

00:29:45.630 --> 00:29:52.060
say, '98, which is a relative benchmark, quite
different just from the perspective of the

00:29:52.060 --> 00:29:56.260
amount of foreign exchange reserves they have,
the sophistication of the system.

00:29:56.260 --> 00:30:01.390
I do think longer run it's a real challenge.
And obviously, if we're talking independent

00:30:01.390 --> 00:30:06.990
central banks, don't know quite how to interpret
that in the Russian context. A lot depends

00:30:06.990 --> 00:30:12.360
on sort of where oil goes from here, it seems
to me. There is a -- some degree of confidence

00:30:12.360 --> 00:30:17.120
that we, you know, probably seen the bottom,
at least in terms of where things are likely

00:30:17.120 --> 00:30:21.310
to average going forward. I think that's obviously
a better environment. If that's not right,

00:30:21.310 --> 00:30:25.760
then it becomes -- it becomes much more difficult.
MALLABY: There's a kind of mystery, isn't

00:30:25.760 --> 00:30:30.550
there, about the Russian central bank in the
last three or four months. Which is that they

00:30:30.550 --> 00:30:36.460
begin this crisis with absolutely enormous
reserves. And, of course, you can run through

00:30:36.460 --> 00:30:41.940
reserves fairly quickly. And we know that
central banks like to practice forward guidance

00:30:41.940 --> 00:30:46.820
with respect to monetary policy, but retrospective
guidance with respect to how they spend their

00:30:46.820 --> 00:30:49.210
reserves. They always announce that 'oops,
we've run out'.

00:30:49.210 --> 00:30:53.000
So there's some skepticism about the numbers
when the announced -- when you know that they

00:30:53.000 --> 00:30:58.010
are using reserves, the announced numbers
of reserves are probably exaggerated. I think

00:30:58.010 --> 00:31:01.030
that's a fair rule from the Asia crisis and
from other crises.

00:31:01.030 --> 00:31:04.340
But nonetheless, they had a lot of reserves.
So they're using these reserves. And then

00:31:04.340 --> 00:31:07.700
all of the sudden, they switch tactic and
they say instead of using these reserves,

00:31:07.700 --> 00:31:11.710
we're going to jack up the interest rate to
some level which, given the fact that projections

00:31:11.710 --> 00:31:16.100
on the Russian economy are between negative
3 percent growth for this year and negative

00:31:16.100 --> 00:31:20.230
5 percent growth this year, it isn't particularly
credible, particularly when you have Putin

00:31:20.230 --> 00:31:24.110
in power, that a central banker is going to
get away with holding interest rates that

00:31:24.110 --> 00:31:27.460
high forever.
So why shift away from the tool that ought

00:31:27.460 --> 00:31:32.080
to work -- namely, reserves -- towards the
tool that wasn't credible -- namely, interest

00:31:32.080 --> 00:31:36.860
rates.
ALEXANDER: Again, I -- look, I think -- I

00:31:36.860 --> 00:31:43.820
would say a couple things. First of all, the
-- the magnitude of this crisis for Russia

00:31:43.820 --> 00:31:48.160
has intensified over the period we're talking
about. So there was obviously the initial

00:31:48.160 --> 00:31:51.470
period at the beginning of last summer when
you had sort of sanctions and this was initially

00:31:51.470 --> 00:31:55.213
in its early phases sort of about that. You
then have oil prices which started to fall

00:31:55.213 --> 00:32:00.431
sort of in the middle of the year. And, frankly,
over the course of that, I think people really

00:32:00.431 --> 00:32:03.770
didn't know exactly how far you were going
to go.

00:32:03.770 --> 00:32:08.370
With respect to the -- you know, your initial
utilization of reserves and then switching

00:32:08.370 --> 00:32:12.240
to a different strategy, I think to a certain
extent I think you have to understand that

00:32:12.240 --> 00:32:18.570
in a circumstance where things were deteriorating
quite rapidly, as you kind of -- as oil prices

00:32:18.570 --> 00:32:23.460
fell over the fall, and as the effectiveness
of the sanctions, which sort of surprised

00:32:23.460 --> 00:32:29.330
people, sort of started to hold forth.
I think ultimately how you utilize those is

00:32:29.330 --> 00:32:36.490
-- is something in those circumstances which
in almost any political circumstance is going

00:32:36.490 --> 00:32:41.020
to be -- is going to be more than an issue
for just the central bank. And so look, these

00:32:41.020 --> 00:32:47.140
are -- these are complicated choices. Obviously,
you've got a domestic financial system that

00:32:47.140 --> 00:32:51.550
is to some extent vulnerable in this context.
And the question of how you use it to support

00:32:51.550 --> 00:32:57.630
that as opposed to purely in a macro sense
is a -- number one, a complicated set of choices.

00:32:57.630 --> 00:33:02.350
But it is also one that, frankly, I think
nobody is going to leave purely to a -- to

00:33:02.350 --> 00:33:04.980
a central bank.
Look, the Federal Reserve found in the midst

00:33:04.980 --> 00:33:10.900
of its crisis that there were limits to its
independence; right? There were -- on -- there

00:33:10.900 --> 00:33:14.970
were things that were purely monetary policy.
And then the degree to which you use the central

00:33:14.970 --> 00:33:22.100
bank balance sheet more broadly, it -- you
know, the veil of independence sort of strips.

00:33:22.100 --> 00:33:27.260
So I don't think it's that surprising that
this has been complicated.

00:33:27.260 --> 00:33:33.530
LEVI: Build a tiny bit on this point that
it becomes quite political, given the circumstances.

00:33:33.530 --> 00:33:37.520
If the Russian government without the central
bank acting independently with Putin as a

00:33:37.520 --> 00:33:46.600
major player, needs to support Russian companies
that have -- that have financing problems.

00:33:46.600 --> 00:33:52.780
It's very useful to be able to pick and choose.
It's not just -- set aside the economics of

00:33:52.780 --> 00:33:58.300
it. It's very useful for a leader who is in
potentially weakening political position to

00:33:58.300 --> 00:34:04.450
be able to pick various powerful players in
the economy to reward, to say you're going

00:34:04.450 --> 00:34:08.370
to do well, I'm going to help you out, as
opposed to just operating through these very

00:34:08.370 --> 00:34:13.240
broad macro channels, where you don't necessarily
get the same political payoff.

00:34:13.240 --> 00:34:18.149
MALLABY: Yes. Microphone behind you, I think.
QUESTION: (OFF-MIKE).

00:34:18.149 --> 00:34:21.990
MALLABY: Just look behind you at the microphone.
QUESTION: Oh, I'm sorry. Thank you. Patricia

00:34:21.990 --> 00:34:25.599
Patterson, Patterson Investments.
It seems to me that everything we're talking

00:34:25.599 --> 00:34:30.190
about this morning and everything we've been
talking about for weeks assumes that the Saudis

00:34:30.190 --> 00:34:36.460
will continue their policy and produce full-out.
Can you envision a situation where they might

00:34:36.460 --> 00:34:40.280
decide to reverse that policy and reduce their
production?

00:34:40.280 --> 00:34:45.569
MALLABY: Michael?
LEVI: So I can envision two situations that

00:34:45.569 --> 00:34:51.049
I don't put enormous odds on. At least in,
let's say, the next year.

00:34:51.049 --> 00:34:56.129
So the first is just that prices fall substantially
lower and they decide to step in. And this

00:34:56.129 --> 00:35:00.099
would be consistent with history. I think
one of the biggest misreadings of what's happened

00:35:00.099 --> 00:35:05.720
in recent months is this idea that this is
unprecedented. It's not unprecedented. It's

00:35:05.720 --> 00:35:09.809
approximately what they did the last time
oil prices collapsed. So the last time oil

00:35:09.809 --> 00:35:16.769
prices collapsed in 2008, there was a sort
of OPEC agreement that fell apart within hours

00:35:16.769 --> 00:35:23.839
to cut production when prices were around,
I think, $70 or so. Around $50 was when they

00:35:23.839 --> 00:35:28.450
actually stepped in with substantial cuts.
But they had a very little impact. The price

00:35:28.450 --> 00:35:31.299
kept falling.
And if you were to take a lesson to that,

00:35:31.299 --> 00:35:35.930
it might be 'don't cut production if you don't
think you're going to be successful'. It reduces

00:35:35.930 --> 00:35:40.170
your influence and your credibility. And then
when prices fell substantially further, around

00:35:40.170 --> 00:35:47.190
$30 or $40, there was a very large reduction
in supply out of Saudi Arabia and a few other

00:35:47.190 --> 00:35:51.680
OPEC countries that contributed to halting
the slide.

00:35:51.680 --> 00:35:56.549
So it wouldn't be a shock. I think it would
be a bigger surprise this time, given the

00:35:56.549 --> 00:36:00.450
statements. You've seen the drumbeat of statements
not only out of Saudi Arabia, but others.

00:36:00.450 --> 00:36:04.309
I think it would be a bigger surprise. But
it wouldn't be an absolute shock to see at

00:36:04.309 --> 00:36:07.339
those kinds of prices then do the same thing
again.

00:36:07.339 --> 00:36:15.339
There's also all sorts of speculation about
Saudi leadership and about potential changes.

00:36:15.339 --> 00:36:21.079
And the best you can say is no one knows what
the odds of a big shift are politically. And

00:36:21.079 --> 00:36:25.369
no one really knows what the odds are of a
policy shift that comes from that, or some

00:36:25.369 --> 00:36:29.210
sort of disruption within the country. But
you don't want to, I think, write that off.

00:36:29.210 --> 00:36:36.240
And I'm not an expert on Saudi Arabian intrigue,
but I would say that people who are say don't

00:36:36.240 --> 00:36:43.300
-- don't write this -- don't write this off.
MALLABY: Yes.

00:36:43.300 --> 00:36:53.400
QUESTION: Ron Shelp, author and filmmaker.
When you talk about the Euro bank and maybe

00:36:53.400 --> 00:36:59.329
buying bonds and so forth, it seems to me
as an observer from this side of the Atlantic

00:36:59.329 --> 00:37:04.940
that Chancellor Merkel is the kingmaker. I
mean, she plays a role that's just sort of

00:37:04.940 --> 00:37:10.420
unbelievable. I gather her austerity policies
are still very, very popular. Which makes

00:37:10.420 --> 00:37:17.380
me wonder, as one of you said, just how far
this will go and how long it will last. I

00:37:17.380 --> 00:37:22.800
mean, have I misread the situation? Or could
somebody comment on it? Thank you.

00:37:22.800 --> 00:37:25.880
MALLABY: I seem to remember that Philippe
might have an opinion about Germany.

00:37:25.880 --> 00:37:31.829
LEGRAIN: Well, I mean, my view of the Eurozone
crisis is that it's a crisis largely caused

00:37:31.829 --> 00:37:38.099
by the bad lending of German banks, which
in turn is a function of the mercantilist

00:37:38.099 --> 00:37:43.350
policies that Germany pursues; and that very
successfully, it has managed to shift most

00:37:43.350 --> 00:37:49.430
of the cost of the crisis onto the debtors.
But now, the debtors are not able to pay those

00:37:49.430 --> 00:37:53.740
debts in full.
So you have -- either Germany has to shift

00:37:53.740 --> 00:38:02.250
so that there's a fairer distribution of the
costs, or at some point the south rebels,

00:38:02.250 --> 00:38:11.069
or is forced to default and/or leaves the
euro. And those, I think, are the -- are the

00:38:11.069 --> 00:38:16.521
only ways out.
The reason why I think that -- I mean, you

00:38:16.521 --> 00:38:24.269
would think that if your priority was getting
paid back, that you would be quite relaxed

00:38:24.269 --> 00:38:30.110
about QE engaging in exceptional monetary
-- in the ECB engaging in exceptional monetary

00:38:30.110 --> 00:38:34.089
policy. Because actually, it would increase
the chance of you being paid back. But even

00:38:34.089 --> 00:38:40.769
then, the German taboo about the use of this.
And the false belief that the modernization

00:38:40.769 --> 00:38:46.099
of debt implies losses to German taxpayers
limits even that policy scope.

00:38:46.099 --> 00:38:51.529
So you've got a German government which says
basically, we don't like QE, we don't like

00:38:51.529 --> 00:38:56.309
fiscal expansion, just give us our money back.
And I don't think that's a consistent position.

00:38:56.309 --> 00:39:01.880
So at some point, there's going -- it's going
to break.

00:39:01.880 --> 00:39:09.140
MALLABY: Yes. In the back there.
QUESTION: Hi, Baily Kempner from the Abraaj

00:39:09.140 --> 00:39:11.509
Group.
I was wondering if you could comment on how

00:39:11.509 --> 00:39:18.080
several of these global factors affect emerging
markets. And you might take a view that there's

00:39:18.080 --> 00:39:25.280
a key difference between oil import versus
oil export economies. But, you know, you could

00:39:25.280 --> 00:39:30.170
also argue that as these economies diversify,
that difference is more muted.

00:39:30.170 --> 00:39:37.290
I was actually wondering beyond the oil prices
whether some of those monetary policies -- Russia,

00:39:37.290 --> 00:39:44.150
Greece, et cetera -- how those factors also
come in to play with emerging markets, specifically

00:39:44.150 --> 00:39:49.289
beyond the BRIC countries.
MALLABY: There was an Economist headline a

00:39:49.289 --> 00:39:58.670
little while ago which went, �what Vlad
can learn from Chad�. The idea being that

00:39:58.670 --> 00:40:03.359
oil economies in Africa had suffered less
than Russia. Then if anyone else wants to

00:40:03.359 --> 00:40:07.329
pick up on --
LEVI: Don't get yourself into a war and be

00:40:07.329 --> 00:40:13.079
under financial sanctions.
Look, and Mexico is a nice example of that,

00:40:13.079 --> 00:40:18.760
as well. And -- and more broadly, I'm going
to give you briefly a non-emerging economy

00:40:18.760 --> 00:40:24.119
example. There are a lot of things that matter,
even to oil exporters, other than the price

00:40:24.119 --> 00:40:26.170
of oil.
So if you look at the IMF's revisions a couple

00:40:26.170 --> 00:40:31.289
days ago to growth estimates, the downward
revision to the Canadian growth estimate -- Canada,

00:40:31.289 --> 00:40:37.440
an oil exporter -- was smaller than the downward
revision to the European growth estimates,

00:40:37.440 --> 00:40:42.930
Europe oil importers. Why? Canada's tied to
the United States, generally has a sound economy.

00:40:42.930 --> 00:40:49.200
Europe has a much -- you know, has a much
larger set of problems. So a lot matters there.

00:40:49.200 --> 00:40:54.769
I think there are -- you sort of separated
things. Obviously, there are some oil exporting

00:40:54.769 --> 00:40:59.880
economies where 50 percent of their GDP is
oil. They have no way out of this. At least

00:40:59.880 --> 00:41:04.529
the ones that have large reserves have ways
of dealing with the fiscal -- fiscal challenges.

00:41:04.529 --> 00:41:08.660
On the oil importing side, I think you've
seen interesting developments where not only

00:41:08.660 --> 00:41:14.670
are these countries passively taking advantage
of what's happening, just by lowering their

00:41:14.670 --> 00:41:17.980
import bills, but some of them are taking
advantage of the policy space that's been

00:41:17.980 --> 00:41:23.150
opened up. So a lot of these countries have
been plagued by burdens from fuel subsidies

00:41:23.150 --> 00:41:28.030
for a long time that, you know, economists
will tell you are bad for the economy. They

00:41:28.030 --> 00:41:32.559
don't help the people, the poorer people who
they're really targeted at. But they're politically

00:41:32.559 --> 00:41:37.579
nightmarish to remove. And in part because
you have this -- well, in large part because

00:41:37.579 --> 00:41:43.220
you would have this immediate increase in
fuel cost that people would react badly to.

00:41:43.220 --> 00:41:47.539
But what you've seen is with falling prices,
you can remove some of these subsidies without

00:41:47.539 --> 00:41:51.780
having to have that big, immediate impact.
And so you've seen smart leaders respond in

00:41:51.780 --> 00:41:56.799
India and Indonesia. And there's -- and in
a host of other places where you have subsidy

00:41:56.799 --> 00:42:00.390
forms underway, leaders are looking at reforming
those. And those should help these countries

00:42:00.390 --> 00:42:04.450
be stronger not just now, but for the longer
-- for the longer haul.

00:42:04.450 --> 00:42:08.220
So I think when you look at the impact on
emerging economies, it's -- it's both this

00:42:08.220 --> 00:42:12.670
immediate piece, but also what the leaders
do with some of the political space that's

00:42:12.670 --> 00:42:15.760
created.
LEGRAIN: Yeah. I mean, as an oil importer,

00:42:15.760 --> 00:42:21.210
falling oil prices ought to be good for the
Eurozone. And yet, the economy is so weak

00:42:21.210 --> 00:42:29.819
that the one-off fall in prices is leading
to -- already to reduced wage offers. In effect,

00:42:29.819 --> 00:42:35.200
therefore, entrenching the deflationary expectations.
It's like wage-price spiral in reverse.

00:42:35.200 --> 00:42:39.290
And that tells you that -- the mess the Eurozone
is in. Even when you get some good news, it

00:42:39.290 --> 00:42:43.059
ends up having a negative impact.
MALLABY: Lewis, do you want to comment on

00:42:43.059 --> 00:42:47.329
potential Latin American travel around the
corner?

00:42:47.329 --> 00:42:51.559
ALEXANDER: Sure. Actually, I was going talk
a little bit about sort of monetary --

00:42:51.559 --> 00:42:53.440
MALLABY: OK.
ALEXANDER: -- policy globally and how it affects

00:42:53.440 --> 00:42:56.589
emerging markets.
We do have -- we are at this interesting point

00:42:56.589 --> 00:43:00.859
where the U.S. is likely to tighten while
at the same time we're going to get QE from

00:43:00.859 --> 00:43:06.430
the ECB, ongoing QE from Japan. I think there's
a lot of uncertainty among emerging markets

00:43:06.430 --> 00:43:12.509
about how that is all going to play out.
I think I'm pretty -- I feel quite strongly

00:43:12.509 --> 00:43:16.609
that the Fed is going to be slow to adjust
rates. That doesn't necessarily mean they

00:43:16.609 --> 00:43:21.690
will be late to adjust rates. But it will
-- it will not be an aggressive move. I think

00:43:21.690 --> 00:43:24.779
that will help those economies that are vulnerable
to that.

00:43:24.779 --> 00:43:31.390
In particular, I would note that one of the
important issues is an awful lot of corporate

00:43:31.390 --> 00:43:36.390
borrowers in the emerging world have borrowed
in dollars. And that has played an important

00:43:36.390 --> 00:43:40.720
part in generating sort of credit growth in
the emerging word.

00:43:40.720 --> 00:43:44.950
I think there's a lot of uncertainty around
how the combined effects of the depreciation

00:43:44.950 --> 00:43:49.841
of the dollar and higher U.S. rates are going
to filter back through that channel. That

00:43:49.841 --> 00:43:56.210
-- if that were the only thing that were happening,
I think one would be potentially quite negative

00:43:56.210 --> 00:44:02.059
about what was going on in that world. I think
in some sense we are -- the emerging world

00:44:02.059 --> 00:44:06.530
benefits to some degree that you're going
to have these other sources of sort of monetary

00:44:06.530 --> 00:44:10.749
stimulus that aren't related to the dollar.
There are still going to be some effects there.

00:44:10.749 --> 00:44:14.930
I think we really don't know how exactly all
these things are going to play out. It is

00:44:14.930 --> 00:44:21.800
one of the important questions for 2015.
I think as you see us transition to the Fed

00:44:21.800 --> 00:44:27.029
hiking -- there's an awful lot of concern
about how this will play out. It doesn't have

00:44:27.029 --> 00:44:31.989
to be a disaster. But it is certainly one
of the uncertainties we're facing this year.

00:44:31.989 --> 00:44:37.400
MALLABY: But at the risk of sounding like
a German policymaker commenting on Italy,

00:44:37.400 --> 00:44:47.480
isn't it the case that in countries such as
Brazil, the commodity boom did provide space

00:44:47.480 --> 00:44:51.300
for the government to take its eye off the
ball, both in terms of microeconomic incentives,

00:44:51.300 --> 00:44:57.259
sort of allowing corruption of the big state-owned
companies and so forth. And in terms of the

00:44:57.259 --> 00:45:00.720
budget, right? They now have to tighten the
fiscal position.

00:45:00.720 --> 00:45:06.829
ALEXANDER: I -- look, absolutely. As is -- as
is always the case with emerging markets,

00:45:06.829 --> 00:45:11.690
generalizations are sort of problematic. It
is not one story. And that's certainly true

00:45:11.690 --> 00:45:16.160
when you look at Latin America.
I would very much agree that I think Mexico

00:45:16.160 --> 00:45:21.089
in some sense is in better shape. First of
all, is a more important energy producer than

00:45:21.089 --> 00:45:27.279
is Brazil. Although, Brazil has become a very
important energy producer. And I would argue

00:45:27.279 --> 00:45:33.839
to a certain extent under Dilma you have seen
-- there was -- there was a period when, frankly,

00:45:33.839 --> 00:45:39.839
a PT-led government did not make as much progress
as it had sort of early in its life. And to

00:45:39.839 --> 00:45:44.940
a certain extent, that is coming back to cause
problems. And I think there's no question

00:45:44.940 --> 00:45:51.420
that Brazil faces a very serious period of
adjustment going forward. It's not a crisis.

00:45:51.420 --> 00:45:58.950
This is not 1999 or 1994 or one of those other
periods when you had real crises in Brazil.

00:45:58.950 --> 00:46:02.200
But it is going to be a difficult period.
I think when you look around the rest of Latin

00:46:02.200 --> 00:46:07.900
America, you've obviously got Venezuela, which
is a real basket case. And, frankly, it was

00:46:07.900 --> 00:46:13.059
a real basket case at $100 a barrel and at
$50 a barrel it's that much worse. Argentina

00:46:13.059 --> 00:46:18.579
is another one that is a commodity exporter
that is going through serious problems that's

00:46:18.579 --> 00:46:23.450
in addition to all the -- a difficult macroeconomic
environment. You've got a political transition

00:46:23.450 --> 00:46:28.849
that's coming at a difficult time. That's
all going to be difficult.

00:46:28.849 --> 00:46:34.799
So, you know, you've got a series of countries
that are -- are struggling to a greater or

00:46:34.799 --> 00:46:40.270
lesser degree. It is -- it is one of those
things where there are better stories and

00:46:40.270 --> 00:46:45.430
worse stories. But it is in some sense a more
challenging environment for the region as

00:46:45.430 --> 00:46:51.220
a whole. When you combine weaker commodity
prices across the board, higher dollar -- higher

00:46:51.220 --> 00:46:56.700
-- a stronger dollar, higher dollar interest
rates, that is a particularly challenging

00:46:56.700 --> 00:47:02.200
set of circumstances for Latin America.
LEVI: Lewis points at something that is important

00:47:02.200 --> 00:47:06.470
and that's to some extent being lost in the
focus on falling oil prices. And that's that

00:47:06.470 --> 00:47:12.519
a lot of this is a broader phenomenon on commodities
markets, and so affects a broader set of countries.

00:47:12.519 --> 00:47:17.779
And that's part because of a common shock
in demand. There's this huge focus on the

00:47:17.779 --> 00:47:24.380
supply side of things. But weak global demand
is a large part. In the oil space, you know,

00:47:24.380 --> 00:47:30.660
a little short of a million barrel a day reduction
in projected consumption for 2015 on -- just

00:47:30.660 --> 00:47:33.069
on that -- from the slowing of the global
economy.

00:47:33.069 --> 00:47:34.069
(CROSSTALK)
MALLABY: -- China effect?

00:47:34.069 --> 00:47:38.320
LEVI: That is substantially a China effect,
but also a Europe effect.

00:47:38.320 --> 00:47:44.680
And for some food commodities, also you had
a very good crop last year. And so you have

00:47:44.680 --> 00:47:49.570
actually supply-side forces causing problems
in some other commodities markets, and then

00:47:49.570 --> 00:47:52.529
the demand side hitting across the board.
So you have this broader set of countries

00:47:52.529 --> 00:47:57.470
like Argentina and, frankly, this broader
set of issues for a country like Brazil than

00:47:57.470 --> 00:48:00.359
you would have if it were only the oil price
we were talking about.

00:48:00.359 --> 00:48:06.239
MALLABY: I think I saw -- yes. A question
in the middle there.

00:48:06.239 --> 00:48:11.640
QUESTION: Chris Brody, Vantage Partners.
I wonder if you'd talk about the Swiss revaluation.

00:48:11.640 --> 00:48:15.359
Is it significant? Will it impact Europe?
Or is it just a small country and everybody

00:48:15.359 --> 00:48:22.340
knew it was undervalued anyway?
ALEXANDER: I do think it's significant. It's

00:48:22.340 --> 00:48:28.119
already had a big effect on markets. And it
is a -- you know, Switzerland's not a huge

00:48:28.119 --> 00:48:33.489
economy. But it certainly has had a significant
effect if for no other reason, than it's affected

00:48:33.489 --> 00:48:37.410
how risk is priced. You've seen (inaudible)
spike and whatnot.

00:48:37.410 --> 00:48:41.880
I -- just let me comment on this a little
further. I think it's a very interesting phenomenon.

00:48:41.880 --> 00:48:47.700
In some ways, if you go back all the way to
'92 in the ERM crises, we went through a series

00:48:47.700 --> 00:48:52.619
of crises in the '90s that were really about
fixed exchanged rates that ultimately broke

00:48:52.619 --> 00:48:56.519
down. It really started with ERM crises. It
sort of rolled through Mexico in '94. And

00:48:56.519 --> 00:49:00.700
then you had Asia and all of that.
I think through most of that, there was a

00:49:00.700 --> 00:49:06.279
kind of asymmetry about it, where we kind
of assumed that gee, in a world of diminishing

00:49:06.279 --> 00:49:11.779
barriers to capital flows and sort of growing
global capital, it was -- it was very hard

00:49:11.779 --> 00:49:16.869
for a country to defend a country -- an exchange
rate that was under pressure on the downside

00:49:16.869 --> 00:49:19.930
from these global flows.
In some ways what's interesting about Switzerland

00:49:19.930 --> 00:49:26.410
I would argue is essentially, they made a
decision that the potential consequences of

00:49:26.410 --> 00:49:30.930
dealing with it on the other side were so
large, that they didn't want to bear the burden.

00:49:30.930 --> 00:49:35.530
Now, there are lots of specific things that
apply to Switzerland, including the capital

00:49:35.530 --> 00:49:39.150
structure of the Swiss National Bank and who
was ultimately going to bear the consequences

00:49:39.150 --> 00:49:44.859
of that. But I do think it's -- it's indicative
of the fact that we live in a world where

00:49:44.859 --> 00:49:51.380
global capital is just very large and continues
to grow. And it's large relative to other

00:49:51.380 --> 00:49:55.170
things that matter.
So for example, if you think of the fiscal

00:49:55.170 --> 00:50:01.690
capacity of Switzerland to support its financial
institutions in an environment where there's

00:50:01.690 --> 00:50:07.859
this large capital flows potentially coming
in, those institutions could become very large.

00:50:07.859 --> 00:50:14.680
And you get the sense in which the just general
growth of finance has made policies that not

00:50:14.680 --> 00:50:19.050
that long ago you would have assumed would
have been viable, all of the sudden look somewhat

00:50:19.050 --> 00:50:21.900
different.
And so, you know, I don't want to overdo the

00:50:21.900 --> 00:50:26.390
kind of immediate impact of it. But I do think
it's indicative of kind of a world we live

00:50:26.390 --> 00:50:32.980
in, where the capital is very large, it's
very mobile. And it does put constraints on

00:50:32.980 --> 00:50:37.749
-- on policies in ways that perhaps are surprising.
MALLABY: That -- I mean, that is a very important

00:50:37.749 --> 00:50:42.239
question, of course, because it also says
something about the Chinese experience of

00:50:42.239 --> 00:50:48.799
intervening to prevent an appreciation. It
wasn't quite pegged, but it was sort of pegged.

00:50:48.799 --> 00:50:54.220
So in China's case, there was always this
speculation that of course the process of

00:50:54.220 --> 00:51:01.420
fixing the renminbi as it was fixed implied
a massive accumulation of dollar treasury

00:51:01.420 --> 00:51:09.790
assets. And the political debate in the sort
of Chinese, you know, microblogs seemed to

00:51:09.790 --> 00:51:17.910
be of the view that the implied wealth loss
to the Chinese state from accumulating a bunch

00:51:17.910 --> 00:51:23.440
of dollars in the trillions, which are going
to be losing value when finally the renminbi

00:51:23.440 --> 00:51:28.770
exploded, that this -- this was supposed to
be a reason for them to rethink their policy.

00:51:28.770 --> 00:51:34.510
But it never seemed to actually push to the
point where it weighed that heavily in the

00:51:34.510 --> 00:51:40.290
government's decisions. It was always a discussion.
It was never -- it never proved to be a decisive

00:51:40.290 --> 00:51:42.670
factor.
As I listen to you about Switzerland, I think

00:51:42.670 --> 00:51:49.900
what you're saying is that in the Swiss case,
the open-ended accumulation of external assets,

00:51:49.900 --> 00:51:55.289
of foreign currency assets which ultimately
would lose value, was a factor that -- is

00:51:55.289 --> 00:51:58.080
that -- is that what you're saying, that that's
what caused them to flip?

00:51:58.080 --> 00:52:02.550
ALEXANDER: I -- I think it's hard to know
exactly what drove them. But I think that

00:52:02.550 --> 00:52:06.499
was certainly an element of it. I think there
was probably a financial stability element

00:52:06.499 --> 00:52:11.510
of it, as well, as they think about just blowing
up the Swiss financial system and the potential

00:52:11.510 --> 00:52:15.569
consequences that would come from that if
ultimately the fiscal authorities have to

00:52:15.569 --> 00:52:16.569
stand behind --
(CROSSTALK)

00:52:16.569 --> 00:52:19.700
MALLABY: But you'd blow up the deposit side
of the financial system, not the asset side

00:52:19.700 --> 00:52:21.489
necessarily, would you?
ALEXANDER: Well, but ultimately --

00:52:21.489 --> 00:52:23.130
(CROSSTALK)
MALLABY: Money flows into Switzerland. It

00:52:23.130 --> 00:52:24.240
goes into Swiss accounts.
(CROSSTALK)

00:52:24.240 --> 00:52:27.749
ALEXANDER: Yes. Well, but ultimately, they've
got to do something with that -- those funds.

00:52:27.749 --> 00:52:30.869
And you don't know sort of the magnitude of
the risks they're going to take.

00:52:30.869 --> 00:52:35.950
So yes, I mean, at some level that's the immediate
effect. But ultimately, if you blow up the

00:52:35.950 --> 00:52:41.339
size of the system, it's very hard to know
exactly what risks you're taking on. And ultimately,

00:52:41.339 --> 00:52:45.299
there is a kind of contingent liability for
the fiscal authorities there.

00:52:45.299 --> 00:52:50.009
Look, I -- I think it -- you know, obviously,
Switzerland is a very different case from

00:52:50.009 --> 00:52:55.270
China. But in some sense, we are dealing with
similar things. And partly it is, I would

00:52:55.270 --> 00:53:02.710
just stress the fact, that the absolute magnitudes
of financial intermediation continue to grow.

00:53:02.710 --> 00:53:06.019
And we think a lot about gee, we had this
big crisis and, you know, we had too big to

00:53:06.019 --> 00:53:10.849
fail and don't we want to shrink finance?
Well, it's not happening. And to be perfectly

00:53:10.849 --> 00:53:14.859
frank, I don't think there's any good reason
to expect it to happen. And ultimately, we're

00:53:14.859 --> 00:53:18.489
going to have to sort of deal with this world
where there's just more and more financial

00:53:18.489 --> 00:53:21.700
intermediation.
MALLABY: So the implication is that floating

00:53:21.700 --> 00:53:25.569
rates are more desirable even than they were
before?

00:53:25.569 --> 00:53:28.819
ALEXANDER: Yes, absolutely.
MALLABY: So does that say something about

00:53:28.819 --> 00:53:33.849
the sustainability of the euro?
LEGRAIN: Clearly, the Swiss decision has already

00:53:33.849 --> 00:53:40.489
had an impact on people with leveraged Swissfranc
positions. It's clearly having a significant

00:53:40.489 --> 00:53:45.521
impact on the euro. Because one of the mysteries
was why when the crisis -- at the height of

00:53:45.521 --> 00:53:49.359
the crisis the euro remained so strong. And
the answer is in large part because the Swiss

00:53:49.359 --> 00:53:53.789
National Bank, as well as the Chinese, was
kept on -- kept on buying.

00:53:53.789 --> 00:53:56.819
And your comparison, I think, with the U.S.
and China is quite (inaudible) in the sense

00:53:56.819 --> 00:54:00.569
that there were people in Switzerland saying,
you know, we don't want loads and loads of

00:54:00.569 --> 00:54:08.660
Eurozone assets, especially with -- at the
prospect of a -- more and more Eurozone assets,

00:54:08.660 --> 00:54:12.000
especially if at some point the peg is going
to break. And now the peg has broke and actually,

00:54:12.000 --> 00:54:15.150
you are going to realize the losses on these
assets. But that's -- there you go.

00:54:15.150 --> 00:54:19.809
But I think more important, actually, is what
it says about central bank commitments. I

00:54:19.809 --> 00:54:24.800
mean, this is a central bank which went out
and said we're going to do whatever it takes.

00:54:24.800 --> 00:54:27.710
And central banks are making all sorts of
these commitments throughout the crisis and

00:54:27.710 --> 00:54:31.849
markets have been believing them. And suddenly,
markets have got burned because actually,

00:54:31.849 --> 00:54:35.119
you find out that commitment isn't really
whatever it takes. There's little asterisks

00:54:35.119 --> 00:54:40.630
and a footnote and disclaimer. And I think
that is significant if a market (inaudible)

00:54:40.630 --> 00:54:47.520
across to other central bank commitments.
MALLABY: Another question? Yes, over there.

00:54:47.520 --> 00:54:50.970
QUESTION: Bhakti Mirchandani, One William
Street.

00:54:50.970 --> 00:54:54.509
What are your thoughts on the outlook for
global housing?

00:54:54.509 --> 00:55:04.539
MALLABY: I saw that the President wants to
stimulate further middle class house buying

00:55:04.539 --> 00:55:11.589
brackets leverage in yesterday's speech.
ALEXANDER: I -- I guess I'll -- I'll say one

00:55:11.589 --> 00:55:17.470
thing, which is housing is in some sense not
global. Right? I mean, housing is always a

00:55:17.470 --> 00:55:23.690
kind of regional story. And so you -- you
really have to answer that question regionally.

00:55:23.690 --> 00:55:29.259
There are obviously parts of the global economy
where real estate -- real estate markets are

00:55:29.259 --> 00:55:34.619
very strong and arguably overvalued, and that's
a real risk. And, you know, China would be

00:55:34.619 --> 00:55:39.589
the kind of top of my list on that as places.
I think there are other places where it's

00:55:39.589 --> 00:55:45.430
much less of an issue. I would put the U.S.
in that category, in spite of what the President's

00:55:45.430 --> 00:55:49.060
proposing.
So I -- you know, I think it is -- if you

00:55:49.060 --> 00:55:55.180
look at the parts, particularly of the emerging
world that have seen very rapid increases

00:55:55.180 --> 00:56:00.769
in domestic financial intermediation, a lot
of that's linked to domestic mortgage markets.

00:56:00.769 --> 00:56:06.690
You do see places where that really has sort
of become an issue. But I guess I would start

00:56:06.690 --> 00:56:10.749
by saying I don't think -- I don't think one
should really think of that as a -- as a global

00:56:10.749 --> 00:56:16.269
thing. It really is sort of country by country.
MALLABY: I think we've got time for one more.

00:56:16.269 --> 00:56:23.029
And -- yes. Yes.
QUESTION: Thank you very much. You mentioned

00:56:23.029 --> 00:56:25.229
that Russia --
MALLABY: Could you state your name and --

00:56:25.229 --> 00:56:31.960
QUESTION: Mahesh Kotecha, SCIC. You mentioned,
the moderator, that Russia has two policy

00:56:31.960 --> 00:56:37.729
tools, interest rates and -- and reserves
and that reserves are for effective or, by

00:56:37.729 --> 00:56:42.619
implication, not as effective as interest
rates, given the current situation in Russia.

00:56:42.619 --> 00:56:46.140
But reserves aren't either. They can be spent
pretty quickly.

00:56:46.140 --> 00:56:54.890
How long do you give the Russians? And do
you think that this issue of the fiscal/reserve/economic

00:56:54.890 --> 00:57:01.559
vulnerability will play a role in how Russia
acts on Ukraine and whether Putin survives?

00:57:01.559 --> 00:57:07.140
MALLABY: Well, since I was the one who raised
that, maybe I'll take a shot at it.

00:57:07.140 --> 00:57:11.150
So I guess what I was -- if I remember the
numbers right -- and if somebody remembers

00:57:11.150 --> 00:57:16.859
better, they can correct me. I think, you
know, in last fall central bank reserves in

00:57:16.859 --> 00:57:23.229
Russia were excess of $400 billion. You know,
that is a lot of money. And the rate of use

00:57:23.229 --> 00:57:29.380
-- there had been a period of active and explicit
intervention. The rate of use suggested that

00:57:29.380 --> 00:57:34.480
this could go on at that rate for, I forget,
twelve months, fifteeen months. I mean, it

00:57:34.480 --> 00:57:38.470
was quite a long way from -- there seemed
to be quite a long way to run.

00:57:38.470 --> 00:57:43.390
Now, Lewis is right that, you know, once you
start using them you can accelerate the use.

00:57:43.390 --> 00:57:47.130
If the oil price moves against you massively,
the implied rate at which you're going to

00:57:47.130 --> 00:57:52.349
use them is going to accelerate. And you don't
want to burn through to the last quarter,

00:57:52.349 --> 00:57:56.170
because then people know you're done. They
see the end. They see the whites of your eyes

00:57:56.170 --> 00:58:01.020
and you're going to lose.
So I get that it's not an endless resource.

00:58:01.020 --> 00:58:06.210
It just felt to me that at the moment when
they switched to a patently unsustainable

00:58:06.210 --> 00:58:14.160
interest rate hike, that sort of almost telegraphed
the fact that the reserves -- that fighting

00:58:14.160 --> 00:58:19.099
with the reserves was not credible. And so
just as a matter of tactics, I wondered whether

00:58:19.099 --> 00:58:24.650
that was a mistake.
Anybody else want to say something? I feel

00:58:24.650 --> 00:58:29.999
bad as the moderator having answered the last
question. But if you're content with my answer,

00:58:29.999 --> 00:58:31.849
then we'll wrap it up there. Thank you, everyone,
for coming.

